{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1791706884,"nonce":"zAmFWmwwgYqGEiONRELFVJ6Nokw=","message":"Three more windows of the same kind, found by the later reviews of the field slice. Each needs another thread to build or allocate in the microseconds a reload takes.\n\n- build_obj_rt now reads the class's field count and array once, at its start, and publishes the runtime object at its end. If set_class_fields and the rt.is_null() test in sync_classes fall between the two, on another thread's first build of a class nothing has used, the class keeps a runtime object whose fields_indexes is shorter than its list, and the added field's offset reads out of bounds.\n- build_obj_rt sizes the lookup table from nproto and reads nproto again when it inserts the methods, so a method added by a reload during a first build can overflow the table. This is the slice 3 pattern; the field list is the part that was made to read once.\n- A hl.CArray of a class fixes its stride when it is allocated. The reload asks which classes are held that way when it is checked and again when it is applied, but a CArray made after the second check and before the runtime object is rebuilt still gets the old stride.\n\nAll three close the same way as the issue says: give the lists spare capacity and publish with release and acquire, or make the build and the reload take one lock.","files":null}]}